          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which virulence factor of Streptococcus pyogenes is directly responsible for rhe</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which virulence factor of Streptococcus pyogenes is directly responsible for rheumatic fever through molecular mimicry with cardiac myosin?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Streptolysin O</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Streptolysin O lyses RBCs and leukocytes and serves as a serological marker (ASO titre), but it does not share structural epitopes with cardiac tissue.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>M protein</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>M protein shares molecular epitopes with cardiac myosin, triggering cross-reactive antibodies that attack heart valves 2–4 weeks after untreated pharyngitis — the mechanism of rheumatic carditis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>SPE acts as a superantigen causing massive T-cell activation, leading to scarlet fever or streptococcal toxic shock — not autoimmune carditis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hyaluronidase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hyaluronidase degrades connective tissue ground substance to facilitate bacterial spread through tissues. It plays no role in molecular mimicry.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the virulence factors: M protein has dual roles — anti-phagocytic defence AND molecular mimicry with cardiac myosin, making it the bridge to rheumatic fever."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. M protein's structural similarity to cardiac myosin is the key link between GAS pharyngitis and the delayed non-suppurative complication of rheumatic fever."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
