          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which laboratory test differentiates Streptococcus pneumoniae from other α-haemo</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which laboratory test differentiates Streptococcus pneumoniae from other α-haemolytic streptococci (viridans group)?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Coagulase test</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The coagulase test distinguishes S. aureus (coagulase-positive) from coagulase-negative staphylococci. It is not applicable to streptococcal identification.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CAMP test</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The CAMP test identifies Group B Streptococcus (S. agalactiae) by demonstrating enhanced haemolysis near S. aureus — it does not differentiate pneumococcus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Optochin sensitivity test</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>S. pneumoniae is uniquely sensitive to optochin (ethylhydrocupreine), showing a zone of inhibition ≥14 mm. Viridans streptococci are optochin-resistant — this is the standard differentiating test.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bile esculin test</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bile esculin hydrolysis identifies Group D streptococci and enterococci (which grow in 40% bile and hydrolyse esculin), not pneumococcus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: both pneumococcus and viridans streptococci show α-haemolysis on blood agar. Optochin sensitivity (pneumococcus = sensitive, viridans = resistant) is the key differentiator."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Optochin sensitivity (along with bile solubility) is the classic test that separates S. pneumoniae from the morphologically similar viridans streptococci."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
