          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following best explains why no effective vaccine exists against the</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following best explains why no effective vaccine exists against the common cold caused by Rhinovirus?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The virus undergoes frequent antigenic shift through genome reassortment</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Antigenic shift (reassortment of segmented genomes) is characteristic of Influenza A, not Rhinovirus. Rhinovirus diversity arises from having over 160 stable serotypes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rhinovirus exists in more than 160 antigenic serotypes</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>With over 160 distinct serotypes and no shared cross-protective surface antigen, a single vaccine cannot elicit broad immunity — this antigenic diversity is the fundamental barrier.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rhinovirus integrates into host chromosomal DNA, evading adaptive immunity</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Rhinovirus is a positive-sense ssRNA virus (Picornaviridae) that replicates entirely in the cytoplasm. It does not integrate into host DNA — that is a retroviral mechanism.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The virus selectively infects and destroys B lymphocytes, preventing antibody formation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Rhinovirus infects nasopharyngeal epithelial cells via the ICAM-1 receptor. Adaptive immunity does develop but remains serotype-specific, unable to cover all 160+ variants.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key point: Rhinovirus has >160 serotypes. Immunity after infection is serotype-specific, so no single vaccine formulation can protect against all strains."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The sheer antigenic diversity of Rhinovirus (>160 serotypes, each immunologically distinct) makes a universal vaccine impractical with current technology."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
