          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A throat swab smear from a 6-year-old child with a greyish-white tonsillar membr</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A throat swab smear from a 6-year-old child with a greyish-white tonsillar membrane shows bacilli with blue-black metachromatic granules against a yellowish-green background. Which stain and organism does this appearance indicate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Gram stain — Streptococcus pyogenes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Gram stain would reveal GAS as Gram-positive cocci arranged in chains — not bacilli with coloured granules. GAS causes exudative pharyngitis, not a pseudomembrane.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>KOH mount — Candida albicans</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>KOH mount dissolves epithelial cells to reveal pseudohyphae and budding yeast cells of Candida — not bacilli with metachromatic granules.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Albert stain — Corynebacterium diphtheriae</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Albert stain demonstrates volutin (polyphosphate) granules as blue-black bodies in yellowish-green bacilli — pathognomonic for C. diphtheriae. The membranous tonsillitis fits diphtheria clinically.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>ZN stain — Mycobacterium tuberculosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ZN stain shows acid-fast bacilli as red rods against a blue background. It is used for sputum/BAL in suspected pulmonary TB, not for throat swab examination.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the three throat swab stains: Gram stain (cocci in chains = GAS), Albert stain (blue-black granules in green bacilli = C. diphtheriae), KOH mount (pseudohyphae = Candida)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Blue-black metachromatic granules in yellowish-green bacilli on Albert stain confirm C. diphtheriae — correlate with the clinical finding of a pseudomembrane that bleeds on removal."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
